@id     03_loops
@title  Loops
@topic  for loops
@lang   c
@show   values

@theory
A for loop repeats code a known number of times:

    for (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {
        printf("%d\n", i);
    }

This prints 1, 2, 3. The three parts are: start (int i = 1), condition (i <= 3), step (i++).

To add numbers up, keep a running total in a variable that starts at 0.

Your task: read n and print the sum 1 + 2 + ... + n.
For n = 5 the answer is 15. For n = 0 the answer is 0.

@starter
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int n;
    scanf("%d", &n);

    int sum = 0;
    // add 1..n to sum

    printf("%d\n", sum);
    return 0;
}

@solution
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int n;
    scanf("%d", &n);
    int sum = 0;
    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) sum += i;
    printf("%d\n", sum);
    return 0;
}

@test
@input
5
@expected
15

@test
@input
1
@expected
1

@hidden_test
@input
0
@expected
0

@hidden_test
@input
100
@expected
5050

#! ------------------------------------------------------------------
#! Steps: Understand -> Practice. The task above is the Practice step.
#! ------------------------------------------------------------------

@step watch
@title  Watch a loop
@code
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    for (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {
        printf("%d\n", i);
    }
    printf("done\n");
    return 0;
}
@text
Press Next line and watch i change in every round of the loop.
A for loop has three parts: the start (int i = 1), the condition checked before every round (i <= 3) and the step after every round (i++).

@step predict
@title  Predict a value
@code
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int sum = 0;
    for (int i = 1; i <= 4; i++) {
        sum += i;
    }
    printf("%d\n", sum);
    return 0;
}
@line   8
@expr   sum
@text
What is sum after line 8 runs?
sum starts at 0 and the loop adds 1, 2, 3 and 4 to it.

@step predict
@title  Predict the output
@code
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
        printf("Hi ");
    }
    printf("\n");
    return 0;
}
@text
What does this program print?

@step choose
@title  Count the rounds
@code
for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    printf("round\n");
}
@text
How many times does this loop print round?
@option 4
@option 5
@option 6
@option It never stops
@correct 2
@answer
i takes the values 0, 1, 2, 3 and 4. When i becomes 5, i < 5 is false and the loop ends.

@step find_bug
@title  Find the bug
@text
Fix the loop so it prints every number from 1 to n.
For n = 3 it should print 1, 2 and 3, but it stops after 2.
@starter
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int n;
    scanf("%d", &n);
    for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
        printf("%d\n", i);
    }
    return 0;
}
@editable 6-6
@solution
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int n;
    scanf("%d", &n);
    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
        printf("%d\n", i);
    }
    return 0;
}
@test
@input
3
@expected
1
2
3
@hidden_test
@input
1
@expected
1

@step explain
@title  Explain it
@text
What would happen if you forgot the i++ part of a for loop?
@rubric
never; changes; forever; infinite
@answer
i would never change, so the condition would stay true and the loop would run forever
(an infinite loop). CodeLens stops a program that runs longer than 3 seconds.

